  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed, defined as assigning a greater portion of probability to unfavorable (here, more negative or less favorable) outcomes compared to favorable ones. In this decision problem, both lotteries involve only zero or negative outcomes (losses or nothing), so the 'favorable' outcome would be the one with the smaller loss (i.e., closer to zero). Lottery A has an 80% chance of 0 and 20% chance of -4000; Lottery B has a 75% chance of 0 and 25% chance of -3000. The unfavorable outcome in A is larger in magnitude (-4000 vs -3000), but less probable (20% vs 25%). To assess skewness: right-skew in this context (with losses) would imply higher probability on the worse (more negative) outcomes. However, since both lotteries have only one non-zero outcome (a loss), and the rest are zero, the skewness can be assessed by comparing the probability-weighted distribution. Lottery A assigns lower probability to a larger loss, making it less right-skewed than Lottery B, which assigns higher probability to a smaller loss. Thus, the more right-skewed lottery is B, because it concentrates more probability (25%) on a loss (unfavorable outcome) compared to A (20%), even though the loss is smaller. The reason would prefer B if B is more right-skewed. However, technically, skewness for discrete two-point distributions can be computed, but intuitively, since both have only one negative outcome, the one with higher probability of loss is more right-skewed (as more mass is on the left tail, but in loss terms, this means more weight on negative side). Given the definition provided (greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes), B has 25% on unfavorable, A has 20%, so B is more right-skewed. Thus, the reason would prefer B. The reason is applicable.",
