  "step_3": "The decision reason requires assessing and preferring the more right-skewed lottery, defined as having a greater portion of probability on unfavorable outcomes. In this case, B has a higher probability of an unfavorable outcome (25% vs 20%), so it is more right-skewed and would be preferred by the reason. The individual does consider the higher probability of loss in B and the smaller loss amount, which are the components needed to assess skewness. However, they do not indicate a preference for higher probability of loss per se, nor do they frame their reasoning in terms of distribution shape or skewness. Instead, they present the difference as a trade-off, implying they might be weighing risk against severity. The reason’s rule is to prefer more right-skewed (i.e., higher probability of loss), but the individual does not express a clear preference for higher probability of loss — they merely state it as a downside offset by a smaller loss. This suggests they are not using skewness as a decision rule, but rather a compensatory trade-off between probability and magnitude. While the relevant information (probabilities and outcomes) is considered, the rule (prefer more right-skewed) is not reflected in the justification. Therefore, it is unlikely the individual used the skewness-based reason, even though they referenced the inputs.",
  "final_assessment_score": 30
